systemd: settle is_mount_point_at() with mount ids when st_dev cannot

Patch 0006 already saw that STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT needs Linux 5.8 and gave
is_mount_point_at() a fallback for when xstatx_full() answers -EUNATCH.  The
fallback it chose was the pre-statx() test: compare an inode's st_dev against its
parent's.  Its own comment names the limitation -- "this cannot see a bind mount
that stays within one file system" -- and stage-2 attempt 7 walked into exactly
that, plus a second limitation the comment does not mention.

Attempt 7 is the first boot where 0010, 0011 and 0012 are all in the deployed
build rather than swapped in over the latch.  It reaches "degraded" with 9 failed
units and 20 remaining "Failed at step NAMESPACE".  Step FDS is at 0, step
CREDENTIALS is at 0 and "does not support 'STATX_MNT_ID'" is at 0, so 0010, 0012
and 0011 are each confirmed and what is left is a different cause.  With PID 1
raised to debug via SIGRTMIN+22, 15 of the 20 state it:

  dhcpcd.service: statx() does not support 'STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT' attribute (running on an old kernel?)
  dhcpcd.service: Failed to determine whether /run/systemd/mount-rootfs/etc/resolv.conf is already a mount point: Protocol driver not attached

/etc/resolv.conf is a regular file, and 0006's fallback opens with O_DIRECTORY,
so it returns -ENOTDIR and 0006 re-raises the original -EUNATCH.  The other
recurring path, /proc/sys/kernel/domainname from ProtectKernelTunables=, is a
regular file too.  Lifting O_DIRECTORY on its own would not help: what
namespace.c is asking about is a read-only bind mount of a file onto itself
inside one file system, and st_dev is shared across such a mount, so the test
would answer "not a mount point" with no way to tell that apart from a real
answer.

Mount ids do answer.  Every inode inside a mount carries that mount's id, so an
inode's id differs from its parent directory's exactly when it is the root of a
mount: it holds for any inode type and it sees a same-file-system bind mount.
path_get_mnt_id_at() works on 4.9 because 0002 and 0011 already recover the
old-style mount id from /proc/self/fdinfo when statx() reports none; without
those two this patch would have nothing to stand on.

That dependency is also why this does not simply replace 0006's test.  Reading
the mount id means reading /proc/self/fdinfo, and mount_setup() -- the earliest
caller, and the one whose failure froze PID 1 in stage-2 attempt 1 -- asks
whether /proc is a mount point before mounting /proc.  So the order is: st_dev
first, because it needs nothing but fstatat() and a positive from it is
definitive, since crossing st_dev means crossing a mount; mount ids second, for
the cases where st_dev returned a negative that is really an "I cannot tell";
and if neither can answer, the first test's error is returned unchanged, so a
caller keying on -ENOENT -- namespace.c does, for its ignore-missing entries --
still sees it.  Boots that never reach the new code path behave exactly as they
did under 0006.

The parent is taken off the path fd_get_path() reads back, not off the caller's
path.  That settles AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW once, at the open, and what comes back
carries no symlinks any more, so resolving the parent cannot land on a different
inode than the one just measured.  "/" is answered directly, since it has no
parent to compare against and is always a mount root.

Upstream is unlikely to take this, for the same reason 0002 and 0011 will not be
taken: systemd's baseline is far above 4.9.

--- a/src/basic/mountpoint-util.c
+++ b/src/basic/mountpoint-util.c
@@ -239,13 +239,16 @@
         return TAKE_PTR(fh_copy);
 }
 
-static int is_mount_root_by_parent(int dir_fd, const char *path, int flags) {
+static int is_mount_root_by_stdev(int dir_fd, const char *path, int flags) {
 
         /* STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT needs Linux 5.8. Below that the attribute is simply never reported, so
          * xstatx_full() gives up with -EUNATCH and every caller sees a hard error. Fall back to the test
          * systemd itself used before statx() existed: a directory is the root of a mount if its st_dev
-         * differs from its parent's, or if it is its own parent. This cannot see a bind mount that stays
-         * within one file system, which is why it stays a fallback and not the primary path. */
+         * differs from its parent's, or if it is its own parent.
+         *
+         * A positive from this is definitive -- crossing st_dev means crossing a mount -- and it needs
+         * nothing but fstatat(), which is why it stays first. A negative is not definitive, and
+         * is_mount_root_by_parent() below carries on for that case. */
 
         _cleanup_close_ int fd = -EBADF;
         struct stat a, b;
@@ -268,6 +271,86 @@
                 return true;
 
         return a.st_ino == b.st_ino;
+}
+
+static int is_mount_root_by_mnt_id(int dir_fd, const char *path, int flags) {
+
+        /* Compares the mount id of an inode against the mount id of its parent directory. Every inode
+         * inside a mount carries that mount's id, so the two differ exactly when the inode is the root of a
+         * mount -- which is the question being asked, holds for any inode type, and unlike st_dev sees a
+         * bind mount that stays inside one file system.
+         *
+         * path_get_mnt_id_at() can answer on this kernel because 0002 and 0011 recover the old-style mount
+         * id from /proc/self/fdinfo when statx() reports none. That is also this test's one cost: it needs
+         * /proc, and mount_setup() asks about /proc before mounting it. Hence it runs second, after the
+         * st_dev test that needs nothing, and a failure here is not an answer. */
+
+        _cleanup_close_ int opened = -EBADF;
+        _cleanup_free_ char *resolved = NULL, *parent = NULL;
+        int r, fd, mnt_id, parent_mnt_id;
+
+        assert(wildcard_fd_is_valid(dir_fd));
+
+        if (isempty(path)) {
+                /* No path to name a parent from, and no fd to read one out of either. A caller that gets
+                 * here with AT_FDCWD or XAT_FDROOT and no path is already answered by the shortcuts at the
+                 * top of is_mount_point_at(). */
+                if (dir_fd < 0)
+                        return -EUNATCH;
+
+                fd = dir_fd;
+        } else {
+                opened = xopenat(dir_fd, path,
+                                 O_PATH|O_CLOEXEC|(FLAGS_SET(flags, AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW) ? 0 : O_NOFOLLOW));
+                if (opened < 0)
+                        return opened;
+
+                fd = opened;
+        }
+
+        /* Take the parent off the path the kernel resolved to, rather than off the caller's. That settles
+         * AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW once, and what comes back carries no symlinks any more, so resolving the parent
+         * with AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW below cannot land on a different inode than the one just measured. */
+        r = fd_get_path(fd, &resolved);
+        if (r < 0)
+                return r;
+
+        if (path_equal(resolved, "/"))
+                return true;
+
+        r = path_extract_directory(resolved, &parent);
+        if (r < 0)
+                return r;
+
+        r = path_get_mnt_id_at(fd, NULL, &mnt_id);
+        if (r < 0)
+                return r;
+
+        r = path_get_mnt_id_at(AT_FDCWD, parent, &parent_mnt_id);
+        if (r < 0)
+                return r;
+
+        return mnt_id != parent_mnt_id;
+}
+
+static int is_mount_root_by_parent(int dir_fd, const char *path, int flags) {
+        int r, q;
+
+        /* Cheapest first, and the only one that works before /proc is mounted. */
+        r = is_mount_root_by_stdev(dir_fd, path, flags);
+        if (r > 0)
+                return r;
+
+        /* Everything else is inconclusive rather than negative: st_dev is shared across a bind mount that
+         * stays inside one file system, and -ENOTDIR only says the test could not look at a non-directory.
+         * Both are what the units on this port hit, so ask the mount ids before believing the answer. */
+        q = is_mount_root_by_mnt_id(dir_fd, path, flags);
+        if (q >= 0)
+                return q;
+
+        /* The mount ids could not answer either. Report what the first test said, errno and all, so that a
+         * caller keying on -ENOENT -- namespace.c does, for its ignore-missing entries -- still sees it. */
+        return r;
 }
 
 int is_mount_point_at(int dir_fd, const char *path, int flags) {
@@ -294,8 +377,9 @@
                         &sx);
         if (r == -EUNATCH) {
                 /* Kernel too old to report STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT. Only take the parent comparison when it
-                 * can actually answer -- it needs a directory -- and otherwise report the original
-                 * error, rather than claiming something is not a mount point when we simply cannot tell. */
+                 * can actually answer -- -ENOTDIR now means neither the st_dev nor the mount id test could
+                 * look -- and otherwise report the original error, rather than claiming something is not a
+                 * mount point when we simply cannot tell. */
                 r = is_mount_root_by_parent(dir_fd, path, flags);
                 if (r != -ENOTDIR)
                         return r;
